  "title": "Default to Chrome for Testing without its infobar or keychain prompts; capture screenshots without a clip",
  "user": { "login": "konard", "id": 1431904, "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4", "gravatar_id": "", "url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard", "html_url": "https://github.com/konard", "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers", "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}", "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}", "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}", "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions", "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs", "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos", "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}", "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events", "type": "User", "user_view_type": "public", "site_admin": false },
  "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR resolves the two open issues, #179 and #180, along with their parent \"address all\" issues, #181 and #183, and this issue, #185. Each fix is made in JavaScript, Python and Rust, and the shared parity catalogue and docs are updated with it.\n\nFixes #185\nFixes #179\nFixes #180\nFixes #181\nFixes #183\n\n#181 and #183 are tracking issues. Their only requirement is to resolve #179 and #180 (and, for #183, #181) in one pull request, and this PR does that.\n\n## #179: Chrome for Testing by default, with no infobar and no keychain prompts\n\n| Requested in #179 | Implementation | Tests |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `resolveLaunchExecutable()` starts Chrome for Testing by default, not the user's Google Chrome | **JS:** `js/src/browser/launch-executable.js` uses Playwright's or Puppeteer's download. Selenium uses one of those, and otherwise asks Selenium Manager for `--force-browser-download`. **Python:** `browser/launch_executable.py` uses Playwright's download, otherwise Selenium Manager's. **Rust:** `launch_executable.rs` takes the path Playwright or Puppeteer reports through Node, otherwise the newest download in the caches. In every language, Google Chrome starts only for `channel: 'chrome'` or an `executablePath`. With no Chrome for Testing installed, the launch fails and the error gives the install command; it never falls back to Google Chrome. | `launch-executable.test.js`, `launcher.test.js`, `webdriver.test.js`, `test_issue_179_chrome_for_testing.py`, `test_launch_browser.py`, `launch_executable_tests.rs`, `engine_launch_tests.rs` |\n| Default restriction `no-chrome-for-testing-infobar` (`--test-type=gpu`) for Chrome for Testing | New restriction and a new `chrome-for-testing` preset in the shared `launch-restrictions.json`. `defaultRestrictionsFor(path, requested)` / `default_restrictions_for` applies the preset only when `isChromeForTesting(path)` matches. Any other browser keeps the command line a person would type. `defaultRestrictions: []` turns the preset off. The session reports what was added as `defaultRestrictions`, and `measureParity()` counts those switches as requested. | Same unit suites, plus `parity.test.js`, `real-browser.test.js` and `real_browser_tests.rs` |\n| `--use-mock-keychain` by default for Chrome for Testing | Part of the same preset. Profile migration (`migrateFrom`) now encrypts with the key of the browser that actually starts: Chromium's fixed mock key, or Chrome for Testing's own \"Chromium Safe Storage\" entry. Before, it used Google Chrome's key, so migrated passwords and cookies did not decrypt. | `os-crypt-keys.test.js`, `test_os_crypt_keys.py`, `rust/src/browser/migration/tests/os_crypt_keys.rs` |\n| Tests for each | `js/tests/e2e/chrome-for-testing.e2e.test.js` starts each JS engine headfully. It reads `chrome://version` to check the executable and both switches. It also measures that the window launched with `defaultRestrictions: []` is taller by the infobar's height. The `Browser Parity` workflow now installs each engine's Chrome for Testing and runs this suite. | CI `Engine lifecycle suites (real Chrome)` |\n\nProblems found and fixed along the way:\n\n- **ChromeDriver version.** Selenium resolved ChromeDriver for the installed Google Chrome, and that driver refuses Chrome for Testing. This affected JS real launches and hand-off reattach, Python real launches, and Rust/fantoccini. The driver is now resolved for the browser that was started (Selenium Manager `--browser-path` / `binary_location`). A `chromedriver` on `PATH` is never used for Chrome for Testing.\n- **Linux sandbox.** On Ubuntu 23.10+ (including `ubuntu-24.04` runners), Chrome for Testing has no AppArmor profile and aborts with \"No usable sandbox!\". The library does **not** add `--no-sandbox` by itself, to keep the command line honest. Instead:\n  - [`docs/chrome-for-testing.md`](docs/chrome-for-testing.md) documents the host fixes;\n  - the engine-suites CI job runs `sysctl kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0`.\n\nDocs: [`docs/chrome-for-testing.md`](docs/chrome-for-testing.md) (new), `docs/feature-parity.md`, `docs/quiet-chrome.md`, and the three READMEs.\n\n## #180: trace and default screenshots are taken without a `clip`\n\n- **Cause.** Trace checkpoint screenshots used the engine's `page.screenshot()`. For the viewport, Playwright sends `Page.captureScreenshot` with a `clip`, and Chrome emulates that size while it captures. A window resized during the capture can stay drawn at the old size.\n- **Fix.** Trace checkpoints, and default captures of a page that has no fixed viewport, now send CDP `Page.captureScreenshot` without a `clip`. This is in `js/src/traces/recorder.js`, `js/src/capture/screenshot.js`, `python/.../capture.py`, `python/.../traces/engine.py`, `rust/src/capture.rs` and `rust/src/traces/page.rs`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `js/tests/unit/traces/issue-180-screenshots.test.js`, `python/tests/unit/traces/test_issue_180_screenshots.py` and the Rust stub-engine tests assert that no `clip` is sent.\n  - `experiments/issue-180/resize-during-screenshot.mjs` reproduces the resize race against a real browser.\n\n## How to reproduce\n\n- **#179:** on `main`, run `launchBrowser({ engine: 'playwright' })` on a machine with Google Chrome installed. It starts `/Applications/Google Chrome.app` (or `/usr/bin/google-chrome`). Now it starts `chrome-*/chrome` from the Playwright cache, and `chrome://version` shows `--test-type=gpu --use-mock-keychain`. `experiments/issue-179/` holds the probes.\n- **#180:** run `node experiments/issue-180/resize-during-screenshot.mjs`.\n\n## CI fixes needed for this PR (these also fail on `main`)\n\n- **Security:** `chore(deps)` takes the rustls-webpki, launch-editor and markdown-it advisories that were published on 2026-10-20.\n- **CI Workflow Policy:** zizmor's `ref-version-mismatch` fired at `safari.yml:79` because upstream moved `v2` of `Swatinem/rust-cache`. The comment now names the exact version the SHA points to, `v2.9.2`.\n- **Broken Link Checker:** `chromium.googlesource.com` answered 503 and then let the re-check time out. A 5xx followed by a timeout is now reported as transient, not gone. Test: \"does not fail for a host that answered 5xx and then timed out\".\n\n## CodeQL notes\n\nCodeQL flags `rust/hard-coded-cryptographic-value` at `rust/src/browser/migration/os_crypt_keys.rs:173,175` and in its tests. The flagged strings are `mock_password` and `peanuts`. These are Chromium's public, fixed passwords for the mock keychain and the Linux basic store, not secrets. `main` uses the same constants in `browser_cookies.rs` and `os_crypt_keys.rs`, and the alerts on those lines (for example #35, #67 and #68) are dismissed as false positives. These new alerts should be dismissed the same way.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- **JS:** `npm test`, `npm run lint`, `npm run format:check`, `npm run check:duplication`, and the changeset validation.\n- **Python:** `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `mypy src`, and the pytest unit suite.\n- **Rust:** `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -Dwarnings`, `cargo test`, and the file-size check.\n- **Repo:** file line limits, `generate-feature-parity.mjs --check`, actionlint, and zizmor.\n\nRelease notes:\n\n- `js/.changeset/chrome-for-testing-default.md` (minor)\n- `python/changelog.d/179.*.md` and `180.fixed.md`\n- `rust/changelog.d/20261021_160000_chrome_for_testing_default.md` (minor)\n",
